1
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:10,200
Think of a matrix W. It turns the unit circle into an ellipse.

2
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:14,100
Let's examine the singular value decomposition of W.

3
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:19,875
It says any matrix is a combination of three steps, rotate, stretch the axes,

4
00:00:19,875 --> 00:00:20,700
and rotate.

5
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:24,445
First, a rotation turns the arms onto the axes.

6
00:00:24,445 --> 00:00:27,633
That is the V transpose matrix from SVD.

7
00:00:27,633 --> 00:00:31,116
Next, sigma stretches each axis by its own amount,

8
00:00:31,116 --> 00:00:36,133
here one point three and zero point five. The circle becomes an ellipse.

9
00:00:36,133 --> 00:00:38,561
Finally, U rotates it into place.

10
00:00:38,561 --> 00:00:44,300
Since rotations never change lengths, all the stretching takes place in sigma.

11
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:49,616
The two stretch factors, sigma one and sigma two, are the half-axes of the ellipse.

12
00:00:49,616 --> 00:00:51,666
They are called singular values.

13
00:00:51,666 --> 00:00:57,853
If every singular value were exactly one, only the rotations would remain.

14
00:00:57,853 --> 00:01:02,033
Nothing gets stretched, and W would be orthogonal.

15
00:01:02,033 --> 00:01:07,366
The singular value decomposition lets us watch the stretching along each natural

16
00:01:07,366 --> 00:01:10,633
direction of the matrix, one direction at a time.

17
00:01:10,633 --> 00:01:13,033
In class we worried about one thing,

18
00:01:13,033 --> 00:01:16,833
that the overall scale of the gradients does not explode.

19
00:01:16,833 --> 00:01:19,899
That is the idea behind Xavier initialization.

20
00:01:19,899 --> 00:01:24,029
But that is a single number, a rough average over the whole matrix.

21
00:01:24,029 --> 00:01:26,433
It says little about any one direction.

22
00:01:26,433 --> 00:01:28,764
Xavier does not solve every problem.

23
00:01:28,764 --> 00:01:34,204
The average can be fine while one direction still stretches too far and another gets

24
00:01:34,204 --> 00:01:35,499
squeezed to nothing.

25
00:01:35,499 --> 00:01:38,041
We want every direction to keep its size,

26
00:01:38,041 --> 00:01:41,699
so none gets stretched too far and none gets squeezed away.

27
00:01:41,699 --> 00:01:45,081
That is why Muon asks for an orthogonal update,

28
00:01:45,081 --> 00:01:50,766
one where every singular value is exactly one. Nothing grows, nothing vanishes.

29
00:01:50,766 --> 00:01:56,833
Every direction moves together, and the matrix stays healthy step after step.

30
00:01:56,833 --> 00:02:02,269
We want every singular value to be one. The SVD hands us exactly that.

31
00:02:02,269 --> 00:02:06,695
Write W as U, sigma, V transpose, keep the two rotations,

32
00:02:06,695 --> 00:02:10,112
and replace every stretch in sigma with one.

33
00:02:10,112 --> 00:02:13,296
What remains is just U times V transpose.

34
00:02:13,296 --> 00:02:18,733
But computing an SVD is a long procedure, and it runs slowly on a GPU.

35
00:02:18,733 --> 00:02:24,654
So we want that same result, U times V transpose, without ever computing the SVD.

36
00:02:24,654 --> 00:02:28,455
What can a GPU do cheaply? It can multiply matrices.

37
00:02:28,455 --> 00:02:31,233
That is the one thing it is built for.

38
00:02:31,233 --> 00:02:33,004
So let's start multiplying.

39
00:02:33,004 --> 00:02:37,532
The only matrix we have is W, so the first thing to try is W times W.

40
00:02:37,532 --> 00:02:42,716
But two matrices can only be multiplied when the number of columns of the first

41
00:02:42,716 --> 00:02:45,407
matches the number of rows of the second.

42
00:02:45,407 --> 00:02:49,344
If W has three rows and two columns, W times W does not fit.

43
00:02:49,344 --> 00:02:54,135
The transpose swaps rows and columns. So W transpose times W always fits.

44
00:02:54,135 --> 00:02:56,366
Let's see what that product gives.

45
00:02:56,366 --> 00:03:00,618
To see it, write W with its SVD, as U, sigma, V transpose.

46
00:03:00,618 --> 00:03:06,629
Transposing a product reverses the order, so W transpose is V, sigma, U transpose.

47
00:03:06,629 --> 00:03:11,173
Now put them side by side. In the middle, U transpose meets U.

48
00:03:11,173 --> 00:03:15,865
The transpose of a rotation is the same rotation done backwards,

49
00:03:15,865 --> 00:03:21,216
and a rotation followed by its reverse does nothing. So the pair cancels.

50
00:03:21,216 --> 00:03:26,787
What is left is V, sigma times sigma, V transpose. That is not what we want.

51
00:03:26,787 --> 00:03:30,966
Our target has U on the left, and here U has disappeared.

52
00:03:30,966 --> 00:03:34,986
So bring U back. Multiply by W one more time, on the left.

53
00:03:34,986 --> 00:03:40,253
Now the V transpose at the end of W meets the V at the start of our product,

54
00:03:40,253 --> 00:03:46,075
and that pair cancels too. What survives is U on the left, V transpose on the right,

55
00:03:46,075 --> 00:03:48,362
and sigma three times in between.

56
00:03:48,362 --> 00:03:51,966
This time both rotations are back where they belong.

57
00:03:51,966 --> 00:03:53,967
And the middle is easy to read.

58
00:03:53,967 --> 00:03:58,164
Sigma is diagonal, which means it only holds the singular values,

59
00:03:58,164 --> 00:03:59,649
one for each direction.

60
00:03:59,649 --> 00:04:03,974
Multiplying diagonal matrices just multiplies the matching entries.

61
00:04:03,974 --> 00:04:08,300
So sigma three times in a row cubes each singular value on its own.

62
00:04:08,300 --> 00:04:12,174
In our example, one point three becomes about two point two,

63
00:04:12,174 --> 00:04:17,533
and zero point five becomes zero point one two five. No direction disturbs another.

64
00:04:17,533 --> 00:04:21,888
Look back at the two attempts. Two copies of W lost a rotation.

65
00:04:21,888 --> 00:04:27,695
Three copies kept both, and only the singular values changed. And we can keep going.

66
00:04:27,695 --> 00:04:32,812
Attach another W transpose times W, and the same cancelling happens again.

67
00:04:32,812 --> 00:04:38,066
U and V transpose stay where they are, and the middle gains two more sigmas.

68
00:04:38,066 --> 00:04:42,746
So the products with an odd number of copies are the ones we can use.

69
00:04:42,746 --> 00:04:46,477
W itself carries sigma. Three copies carry sigma cubed.

70
00:04:46,477 --> 00:04:49,733
Five copies carry sigma to the fifth, and so on.

71
00:04:49,733 --> 00:04:55,110
Every one of these products has the same U on the left and the same V transpose on

72
00:04:55,110 --> 00:04:59,701
the right. So if we multiply each product by a number and add them up,

73
00:04:59,701 --> 00:05:03,570
U and V transpose factor out, and only the middle is a sum.

74
00:05:03,570 --> 00:05:07,833
In that middle, each singular value becomes a number times sigma,

75
00:05:07,833 --> 00:05:10,653
plus a number times sigma cubed, and so on.

76
00:05:10,653 --> 00:05:14,129
A sum of powers like this is a polynomial. Call it p.

77
00:05:14,129 --> 00:05:17,932
So turning W into U times V transpose now means one thing.

78
00:05:17,932 --> 00:05:22,785
We need a p that sends every sigma to one. Can p do that in a single step?

79
00:05:22,785 --> 00:05:25,868
Then it would have to give one for every input.

80
00:05:25,868 --> 00:05:30,917
But p is made of powers of sigma, so a tiny sigma always gives a tiny output.

81
00:05:30,917 --> 00:05:33,737
One step is not enough. So we ask for less.

82
00:05:33,737 --> 00:05:39,246
One step only has to bring sigma closer to one, and then we apply p again and again.

83
00:05:39,246 --> 00:05:44,755
Which p? Every extra term costs more matrix products, so we want as few as possible.

84
00:05:44,755 --> 00:05:46,722
One term alone, a times sigma,

85
00:05:46,722 --> 00:05:50,264
only rescales every singular value by the same number,

86
00:05:50,264 --> 00:05:52,362
so the ellipse stays an ellipse.

87
00:05:52,362 --> 00:05:56,166
So take two terms, a times sigma plus b times sigma cubed.

88
00:05:56,166 --> 00:06:00,760
What should a and b be? We want the repeated steps to settle at one.

89
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:06,233
So a sigma that has reached one must stay there, or the steps would never settle.

90
00:06:06,233 --> 00:06:11,166
Put sigma equals one into p. One cubed is still one, so p gives a plus b.

91
00:06:11,166 --> 00:06:16,166
For one to stay at one, a plus b must equal one. Call this our first wish.

92
00:06:16,166 --> 00:06:21,800
Staying at one is not enough. A sigma that is only close to one has to move closer.

93
00:06:21,800 --> 00:06:27,095
So take a sigma that misses one by a small error e, and put one plus e into p.

94
00:06:27,095 --> 00:06:29,200
We need the cube of one plus e.

95
00:06:29,200 --> 00:06:34,562
Multiplied out, it is one, plus three e, plus terms with e squared and e cubed.

96
00:06:34,562 --> 00:06:39,518
When e is small, those last terms are far smaller still, so we drop them.

97
00:06:39,518 --> 00:06:43,455
For example, one point one cubed is one point three three,

98
00:06:43,455 --> 00:06:45,492
very close to one point three.

99
00:06:45,492 --> 00:06:49,633
So p gives a times one plus e, plus b times one plus three e.

100
00:06:49,633 --> 00:06:54,385
Collect the pieces, and that is a plus b, plus a plus three b times e.

101
00:06:54,385 --> 00:06:57,982
The first piece, a plus b, is one, by our first wish.

102
00:06:57,982 --> 00:07:03,074
So p gives one, plus a plus three b times e. We went in with an error of e,

103
00:07:03,074 --> 00:07:06,875
and we came out with an error of a plus three b times e.

104
00:07:06,875 --> 00:07:10,134
One step multiplies the error by a plus three b.

105
00:07:10,134 --> 00:07:15,700
We want the error to shrink, and the most it can shrink is all the way to nothing.

106
00:07:15,700 --> 00:07:19,366
So our second wish is that a plus three b equals zero.

107
00:07:19,366 --> 00:07:23,267
Two wishes and two unknowns, which is exactly enough.

108
00:07:23,267 --> 00:07:26,506
The second wish says a equals minus three b.

109
00:07:26,506 --> 00:07:30,997
Put that into the first, and minus three b plus b equals one,

110
00:07:30,997 --> 00:07:34,457
so b is minus one half. Then a is three halves.

111
00:07:34,457 --> 00:07:39,168
So p of sigma is three halves sigma, minus one half sigma cubed.

112
00:07:39,168 --> 00:07:42,334
For the matrix, one step is three halves W,

113
00:07:42,334 --> 00:07:45,499
minus one half W times W transpose times W.

114
00:07:45,499 --> 00:07:50,799
That is the Newton–Schulz step, and you could have invented it yourself.

115
00:07:50,799 --> 00:07:56,175
We built p from what happens close to one. But a real sigma can start anywhere.

116
00:07:56,175 --> 00:08:01,278
So take any positive sigma and apply p over and over. Where does it end up?

117
00:08:01,278 --> 00:08:04,680
That is the question in part (e) of the worksheet,

118
00:08:04,680 --> 00:08:07,266
and the rest of this video answers it.

119
00:08:07,267 --> 00:08:10,237
Where does a sigma end up? No theory yet.

120
00:08:10,237 --> 00:08:14,367
Let's just pick a number and try it, say zero point five.

121
00:08:14,367 --> 00:08:20,157
One step of p takes three halves of the number, and subtracts one half of its cube.

122
00:08:20,157 --> 00:08:24,133
Three halves of zero point five is zero point seven five.

123
00:08:24,133 --> 00:08:27,551
Zero point five cubed is zero point one two five,

124
00:08:27,551 --> 00:08:30,969
and half of that is zero point zero six two five.

125
00:08:30,969 --> 00:08:34,667
Subtract, and we get zero point six eight seven five.

126
00:08:34,667 --> 00:08:37,783
Now feed that result back in, and keep going.

127
00:08:37,783 --> 00:08:43,255
Zero point six nine becomes zero point eight seven, then zero point nine eight,

128
00:08:43,255 --> 00:08:47,133
then zero point nine nine nine. It is closing in on one.

129
00:08:47,133 --> 00:08:50,366
That start was below one. Now try one above it.

130
00:08:50,366 --> 00:08:54,974
One point three drops to zero point eight five, which is below one,

131
00:08:54,974 --> 00:09:00,614
and then it climbs, to zero point nine seven, and then zero point nine nine eight.

132
00:09:00,614 --> 00:09:02,333
It also closes in on one.

133
00:09:02,333 --> 00:09:04,991
Both of those were fairly close to one.

134
00:09:04,991 --> 00:09:08,263
So try a start that is far away, down near zero.

135
00:09:08,263 --> 00:09:12,897
Zero point one becomes zero point one five, then zero point two two,

136
00:09:12,897 --> 00:09:17,804
then zero point three three. It is slow at first, but it keeps climbing,

137
00:09:17,804 --> 00:09:20,667
and after eight steps it also reaches one.

138
00:09:20,667 --> 00:09:25,689
All three starts end at one. And once a value is at one, it stays there.

139
00:09:25,689 --> 00:09:29,804
Three halves minus one half is one, so p of one equals one.

140
00:09:29,804 --> 00:09:31,967
That is our first wish at work.

141
00:09:31,967 --> 00:09:34,692
But if some other number also stayed put,

142
00:09:34,692 --> 00:09:40,276
a sigma could get stuck there and never reach one. Is there such a number? Try zero.

143
00:09:40,276 --> 00:09:45,262
Three halves of zero is zero, and zero cubed is zero, so p of zero is zero.

144
00:09:45,262 --> 00:09:48,919
Zero stays put as well. From here on, call the input x.

145
00:09:48,919 --> 00:09:54,303
A value where p of x equals x is called a fixed point. We have found two of them.

146
00:09:54,303 --> 00:09:55,567
Are there any more?

147
00:09:55,567 --> 00:09:59,505
To find them all at once, write down p of x equals x.

148
00:09:59,505 --> 00:10:03,742
That is three halves x, minus one half x cubed, equals x.

149
00:10:03,742 --> 00:10:09,241
Subtract x from both sides, and we get one half x, minus one half x cubed,

150
00:10:09,241 --> 00:10:10,133
equals zero.

151
00:10:10,133 --> 00:10:13,575
Both terms contain one half x, so pull it out.

152
00:10:13,575 --> 00:10:16,793
What is left inside is one minus x squared.

153
00:10:16,793 --> 00:10:21,731
And one minus x squared splits into one minus x, times one plus x.

154
00:10:21,731 --> 00:10:27,867
So the equation says one half x, times one minus x, times one plus x, equals zero.

155
00:10:27,867 --> 00:10:31,479
A product is zero only if one of its factors is zero.

156
00:10:31,479 --> 00:10:34,547
So x is zero, or x is one, or x is minus one.

157
00:10:34,547 --> 00:10:39,523
Those are all the fixed points there are, the two we found and a new one.

158
00:10:39,523 --> 00:10:44,840
And minus one does check out. Minus three halves, plus one half, is minus one.

159
00:10:44,840 --> 00:10:47,226
A singular value is never negative,

160
00:10:47,226 --> 00:10:50,567
so for now minus one is just a point on the list.

161
00:10:50,567 --> 00:10:54,757
But zero matters right away. A sigma at exactly zero is stuck.

162
00:10:54,757 --> 00:11:00,434
And yet zero point one, right next to zero, walked away from it, all the way to one.

163
00:11:00,434 --> 00:11:05,030
Why would one fixed point pull values in and another push them away?

164
00:11:05,030 --> 00:11:10,167
To see what happens around each of them, we need a picture of the iteration.

165
00:11:10,167 --> 00:11:11,921
Start with the graph of p.

166
00:11:11,921 --> 00:11:17,591
Along the bottom is the input x, and the height of the curve above it is the output,

167
00:11:17,591 --> 00:11:20,088
p of x. We know a few points already.

168
00:11:20,088 --> 00:11:23,935
At zero the height is zero, and at one the height is one.

169
00:11:23,935 --> 00:11:29,267
The curve rises from zero, reaches a hump at one, and comes back down after it.

170
00:11:29,267 --> 00:11:33,128
On this picture, one step of the iteration looks like this.

171
00:11:33,128 --> 00:11:36,662
Start at x equals zero point three on the bottom axis,

172
00:11:36,662 --> 00:11:38,757
and go straight up to the curve.

173
00:11:38,757 --> 00:11:43,600
The height you reach is p of zero point three, about zero point four four.

174
00:11:43,600 --> 00:11:48,060
For the next step, zero point four four has to become the new input.

175
00:11:48,060 --> 00:11:52,717
But right now it is a height, and inputs are measured along the bottom.

176
00:11:52,717 --> 00:11:55,800
We need a way to turn a height into a position.

177
00:11:55,800 --> 00:12:00,007
Here is a line that does exactly that, the diagonal y equals x.

178
00:12:00,007 --> 00:12:03,681
Every point on it is as far to the right as it is high.

179
00:12:03,681 --> 00:12:07,488
So go sideways from the curve until you hit the diagonal.

180
00:12:07,488 --> 00:12:10,493
You are still at height zero point four four,

181
00:12:10,493 --> 00:12:14,300
and now you are also above x equals zero point four four.

182
00:12:14,300 --> 00:12:17,196
From there, the second step is the same move.

183
00:12:17,196 --> 00:12:21,058
Go straight up to the curve, which gives zero point six one,

184
00:12:21,058 --> 00:12:23,311
and sideways to the diagonal again.

185
00:12:23,311 --> 00:12:27,880
Keep repeating, and the path climbs like a staircase, zero point eight,

186
00:12:27,880 --> 00:12:30,133
zero point nine five, and into one.

187
00:12:30,133 --> 00:12:31,600
Now a start above one.

188
00:12:31,600 --> 00:12:37,000
From one point two, the curve is below the diagonal, so the first move goes down,

189
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:38,600
to zero point nine four.

190
00:12:38,600 --> 00:12:43,533
After that the path climbs the last little bit and settles on one as well.

191
00:12:43,533 --> 00:12:46,667
Now look at where the curve meets the diagonal.

192
00:12:46,667 --> 00:12:51,667
On the diagonal the height equals x, and on the curve the height is p of x.

193
00:12:51,667 --> 00:12:53,933
So at a crossing, p of x equals x.

194
00:12:53,933 --> 00:12:58,800
The three crossings are our three fixed points, minus one, zero, and one.

195
00:12:58,800 --> 00:13:02,667
And the staircases show which way things move around them.

196
00:13:02,667 --> 00:13:06,400
Near zero the path walks away, and near one it walks in.

197
00:13:06,400 --> 00:13:12,087
Why does one pull values in, while zero pushes them away? For one, we already know.

198
00:13:12,087 --> 00:13:15,651
When we built p, we put in one plus a small error e,

199
00:13:15,651 --> 00:13:19,900
and we chose p so that one step multiplies that error by zero.

200
00:13:19,900 --> 00:13:24,878
So do the same thing at zero. A value near zero is just a small error e.

201
00:13:24,878 --> 00:13:29,510
Put it into p, and we get one point five e, minus one half e cubed.

202
00:13:29,510 --> 00:13:31,723
For a small e, the cube is tiny.

203
00:13:31,723 --> 00:13:36,217
If e is zero point one, e cubed is only zero point zero zero one.

204
00:13:36,217 --> 00:13:38,568
So p gives about one point five e.

205
00:13:38,568 --> 00:13:44,100
This time, one step multiplies the error by one point five, and the error grows.

206
00:13:44,100 --> 00:13:46,185
We have seen this growth already.

207
00:13:46,185 --> 00:13:50,481
Zero point one went to zero point one five, then zero point two two,

208
00:13:50,481 --> 00:13:52,250
then zero point three three.

209
00:13:52,250 --> 00:13:55,788
Each value is about one and a half times the one before.

210
00:13:55,788 --> 00:13:58,063
That is why zero pushes values away.

211
00:13:58,063 --> 00:14:01,601
On the graph, this multiplier is the slope of the curve.

212
00:14:01,601 --> 00:14:05,266
Zoom in at zero, and the curve looks like a straight line.

213
00:14:05,266 --> 00:14:08,678
Move e to the right, and it rises by one point five e.

214
00:14:08,678 --> 00:14:10,700
Rise over run is one point five.

215
00:14:10,700 --> 00:14:11,985
So here is the rule.

216
00:14:11,985 --> 00:14:17,318
Near a fixed point, each step multiplies the error by the slope of the curve there.

217
00:14:17,318 --> 00:14:22,715
If the slope is bigger than one in size, the error grows and values are pushed away.

218
00:14:22,715 --> 00:14:27,213
If it is smaller than one, the error shrinks and values are pulled in.

219
00:14:27,213 --> 00:14:29,526
At zero the slope is one point five,

220
00:14:29,526 --> 00:14:33,510
and zero point zero five drifts to zero point zero seven five,

221
00:14:33,510 --> 00:14:35,566
then zero point one one, and on.

222
00:14:35,566 --> 00:14:38,969
At one, the multiplier is zero, so the slope is zero.

223
00:14:38,969 --> 00:14:41,602
The curve is flat at the top of its hump.

224
00:14:41,602 --> 00:14:44,363
That is our second wish, seen as a picture.

225
00:14:44,363 --> 00:14:47,574
But an error cannot vanish completely in one step.

226
00:14:47,574 --> 00:14:52,582
When we built p, we dropped the terms with e squared, because they were small.

227
00:14:52,582 --> 00:14:54,509
Now they are all that is left.

228
00:14:54,509 --> 00:14:58,747
Worked out, the new error is about one and a half times e squared.

229
00:14:58,747 --> 00:15:04,076
Start at one point two. The error goes from zero point two, to zero point zero six,

230
00:15:04,076 --> 00:15:05,874
to zero point zero zero six.

231
00:15:05,874 --> 00:15:11,204
Each step roughly squares it, which is far faster than shrinking by a fixed factor.

232
00:15:11,204 --> 00:15:14,800
The same calculation at minus one gives the same result.

233
00:15:14,800 --> 00:15:17,930
So zero is unstable, like the top of a hill,

234
00:15:17,930 --> 00:15:21,274
where the smallest push sends you rolling away.

235
00:15:21,274 --> 00:15:25,685
One and minus one are stable, like the bottoms of two valleys,

236
00:15:25,685 --> 00:15:28,033
where everything nearby rolls in.

237
00:15:28,033 --> 00:15:31,156
So far every start rolled into the valley at one.

238
00:15:31,156 --> 00:15:34,406
But the largest start we tried was one point three,

239
00:15:34,406 --> 00:15:39,633
and we don't get to choose sigma. How large can it be before something goes wrong?

240
00:15:39,633 --> 00:15:42,151
Go a little bigger, to one point five.

241
00:15:42,151 --> 00:15:46,261
That is past the hump, where the curve is already coming down,

242
00:15:46,261 --> 00:15:50,172
so the first step drops all the way to zero point five six.

243
00:15:50,172 --> 00:15:55,409
But from there it climbs as before, zero point seven five, zero point nine two,

244
00:15:55,409 --> 00:15:57,066
and into one. Still fine.

245
00:15:57,066 --> 00:16:00,308
A little bigger again, one point eight.

246
00:16:00,308 --> 00:16:04,133
Before we look, where do you think it ends up?

247
00:16:04,133 --> 00:16:08,212
Do the step. Three halves of one point eight is two point seven.

248
00:16:08,212 --> 00:16:12,036
But half of its cube is two point nine two, which is bigger.

249
00:16:12,036 --> 00:16:16,307
Subtract, and the result is negative, minus zero point two one six.

250
00:16:16,307 --> 00:16:20,195
What does p do with a negative number? p has only odd powers,

251
00:16:20,195 --> 00:16:24,530
so flipping the sign of the input just flips the sign of the output.

252
00:16:24,530 --> 00:16:28,482
Plus zero point two one six goes to plus zero point three two,

253
00:16:28,482 --> 00:16:32,752
so minus zero point two one six goes to minus zero point three two.

254
00:16:32,752 --> 00:16:38,107
The negative side is a mirror image of the positive side. Call this the mirror rule.

255
00:16:38,107 --> 00:16:42,760
So this value does what its mirror image would do, with the sign flipped.

256
00:16:42,760 --> 00:16:45,883
It moves away from zero and settles at minus one.

257
00:16:45,883 --> 00:16:50,026
For a singular value, that has the right size but the wrong sign.

258
00:16:50,026 --> 00:16:52,066
This direction ends up reversed.

259
00:16:52,066 --> 00:16:56,720
So what went wrong was the very first step, which landed below the axis.

260
00:16:56,720 --> 00:17:00,211
On the graph, that is where the curve, after its hump,

261
00:17:00,211 --> 00:17:04,866
comes down and crosses the axis. Past that crossing, p of x is negative.

262
00:17:04,866 --> 00:17:09,256
Where is the crossing? Both terms of p contain x over two.

263
00:17:09,256 --> 00:17:12,360
Three halves x is x over two times three,

264
00:17:12,360 --> 00:17:16,221
and one half x cubed is x over two times x squared.

265
00:17:16,221 --> 00:17:20,233
So p of x is x over two, times three minus x squared.

266
00:17:20,233 --> 00:17:24,372
For a positive x, the first factor, x over two, is positive.

267
00:17:24,372 --> 00:17:29,133
So the sign of p comes from the second factor, three minus x squared.

268
00:17:29,133 --> 00:17:33,454
Three minus x squared is positive while x squared is below three,

269
00:17:33,454 --> 00:17:36,180
and negative once x squared passes three.

270
00:17:36,180 --> 00:17:41,566
The change happens at x equals square root of three, about one point seven three.

271
00:17:41,566 --> 00:17:45,583
One point five is below square root of three, and it was fine.

272
00:17:45,583 --> 00:17:49,666
One point eight is just above it, and it flipped. That matches.

273
00:17:49,666 --> 00:17:52,824
Then is every start below square root of three safe?

274
00:17:52,824 --> 00:17:56,225
Look at the curve between zero and square root of three.

275
00:17:56,225 --> 00:18:00,355
It stays above the axis, and its highest point, the top of the hump,

276
00:18:00,355 --> 00:18:03,574
is at height one. So wherever we start in this range,

277
00:18:03,574 --> 00:18:06,733
the first step lands somewhere between zero and one.

278
00:18:06,733 --> 00:18:10,494
And between zero and one, the curve sits above the diagonal.

279
00:18:10,494 --> 00:18:14,820
That means the output is bigger than the input, so every step climbs.

280
00:18:14,820 --> 00:18:19,272
It cannot climb past one, because the curve never goes higher than one.

281
00:18:19,272 --> 00:18:24,099
And the only place where it stops moving is a fixed point, so it ends at one.

282
00:18:24,099 --> 00:18:28,899
So yes. Every sigma between zero and square root of three ends at one.

283
00:18:28,899 --> 00:18:34,780
And exactly at square root of three, the second factor is zero, so p gives zero.

284
00:18:34,780 --> 00:18:39,999
The value lands on the fixed point at zero, and it stays there forever.

285
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:43,817
So past square root of three, the first step flips the sign.

286
00:18:43,817 --> 00:18:47,380
One point eight flipped and still settled, at minus one.

287
00:18:47,380 --> 00:18:52,533
Maybe a flip is all that ever happens. Test that with a much bigger start, three.

288
00:18:52,533 --> 00:18:55,240
Three halves of three is four point five,

289
00:18:55,240 --> 00:19:00,523
and half of three cubed is thirteen point five. Subtract, and we get minus nine.

290
00:19:00,523 --> 00:19:03,362
It flipped, as expected. Now the next step.

291
00:19:03,362 --> 00:19:08,249
By the mirror rule, minus nine does what nine does, with the sign flipped.

292
00:19:08,249 --> 00:19:13,201
Nine goes to thirteen point five minus three hundred sixty four point five,

293
00:19:13,201 --> 00:19:15,776
which is minus three hundred fifty one.

294
00:19:15,776 --> 00:19:19,144
So minus nine goes to plus three hundred fifty one.

295
00:19:19,144 --> 00:19:23,766
It flips every time, and it gets bigger every time. This one explodes.

296
00:19:23,766 --> 00:19:28,265
Both one point eight and three flip, so the difference must be in the size,

297
00:19:28,265 --> 00:19:30,424
meaning the number without its sign.

298
00:19:30,424 --> 00:19:34,803
One point eight came out at size zero point two, smaller than it went in.

299
00:19:34,803 --> 00:19:37,922
Three came out at size nine, bigger than it went in.

300
00:19:37,922 --> 00:19:42,241
So somewhere between them, shrinking turns into growing. Narrow it down.

301
00:19:42,241 --> 00:19:46,800
Two point zero goes to minus one, so its size is one. That is still smaller.

302
00:19:46,800 --> 00:19:50,515
But two point three goes to minus two point six three.

303
00:19:50,515 --> 00:19:53,335
Its size is bigger than where it started.

304
00:19:53,335 --> 00:19:58,633
So the turning point is somewhere between two point zero and two point three.

305
00:19:58,633 --> 00:20:00,890
Where exactly is the turning point?

306
00:20:00,890 --> 00:20:05,662
Compare the size after a step with the size before. Use the factored form.

307
00:20:05,662 --> 00:20:08,886
p of x is x over two, times three minus x squared.

308
00:20:08,886 --> 00:20:14,044
So the size of p of x is the size of x, times the size of three minus x squared,

309
00:20:14,044 --> 00:20:14,625
over two.

310
00:20:14,625 --> 00:20:19,719
Past square root of three, that second part is x squared minus three, over two.

311
00:20:19,719 --> 00:20:24,233
Call it the size factor. Each step multiplies the size by this factor.

312
00:20:24,233 --> 00:20:26,401
Check it with the starts we tried.

313
00:20:26,401 --> 00:20:31,440
At two point zero, the factor is four minus three, over two, which is one half.

314
00:20:31,440 --> 00:20:33,162
And two did go to size one.

315
00:20:33,162 --> 00:20:36,924
At two point three, the factor is about one point one four,

316
00:20:36,924 --> 00:20:39,476
and two point three did come out bigger.

317
00:20:39,476 --> 00:20:44,833
So a factor below one means flip and shrink. A factor above one means flip and grow.

318
00:20:44,833 --> 00:20:48,138
The turning point is where the factor is exactly one.

319
00:20:48,138 --> 00:20:52,504
That means x squared minus three equals two, so x squared equals five.

320
00:20:52,504 --> 00:20:56,683
The turning point is square root of five, about two point two four,

321
00:20:56,683 --> 00:21:01,236
and it does sit between two point zero and two point three. On the curve,

322
00:21:01,236 --> 00:21:06,038
between square root of three and square root of five a step shrinks the size,

323
00:21:06,038 --> 00:21:08,533
and beyond square root of five it grows.

324
00:21:08,533 --> 00:21:12,874
What happens exactly at square root of five? Use the factored form.

325
00:21:12,874 --> 00:21:15,920
x over two, times three minus five, is minus x.

326
00:21:15,920 --> 00:21:19,613
So square root of five goes to minus square root of five.

327
00:21:19,613 --> 00:21:24,278
By the mirror rule, that goes straight back to plus square root of five.

328
00:21:24,278 --> 00:21:27,000
On the picture, the path becomes a square.

329
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:30,572
So square root of five neither settles nor explodes.

330
00:21:30,572 --> 00:21:35,724
It jumps back and forth between plus and minus square root of five forever.

331
00:21:35,724 --> 00:21:40,533
This is called a period two orbit, because it repeats every two steps.

332
00:21:40,533 --> 00:21:44,768
And past square root of five, every step flips and grows.

333
00:21:44,768 --> 00:21:50,490
Two point three goes to minus two point six three, then five point one eight,

334
00:21:50,490 --> 00:21:54,800
then minus sixty one point eight. Like three, it explodes.

335
00:21:55,800 --> 00:21:59,272
So below square root of three, everything goes to one,

336
00:21:59,272 --> 00:22:02,616
and beyond square root of five, everything explodes.

337
00:22:02,616 --> 00:22:07,246
That leaves the gap between them. In the gap, every step flips the sign.

338
00:22:07,246 --> 00:22:12,133
And the size factor is below one, so every step also makes the size smaller.

339
00:22:12,133 --> 00:22:15,834
That suggests a plan. If the size keeps getting smaller,

340
00:22:15,834 --> 00:22:19,601
then sooner or later it drops below square root of three.

341
00:22:19,601 --> 00:22:23,501
And inside square root of three we already know everything.

342
00:22:23,501 --> 00:22:25,748
A positive value goes to plus one.

343
00:22:25,748 --> 00:22:29,383
By the mirror rule, a negative value goes to minus one.

344
00:22:29,383 --> 00:22:33,018
So we only have to follow a start until it gets inside,

345
00:22:33,018 --> 00:22:35,200
and see on which side it arrives.

346
00:22:35,200 --> 00:22:38,962
Try it on the cobweb picture. Start at two point zero.

347
00:22:38,962 --> 00:22:44,050
Go down to the curve, which gives minus one. Then across to the diagonal.

348
00:22:44,050 --> 00:22:49,276
Minus one is inside, on the negative side, and it is already a fixed point.

349
00:22:49,276 --> 00:22:52,900
So two point zero ends at minus one, after one flip.

350
00:22:52,900 --> 00:22:57,079
Now two point two. Down to the curve, at minus two point zero two.

351
00:22:57,079 --> 00:23:01,385
That is smaller in size than two point two, but it is still outside.

352
00:23:01,385 --> 00:23:04,171
So go across to the diagonal and step again.

353
00:23:04,171 --> 00:23:07,907
This time the curve sends it up, to plus one point one one.

354
00:23:07,907 --> 00:23:12,846
Now it is inside, on the positive side, and from there it settles at plus one.

355
00:23:12,846 --> 00:23:15,000
Two flips, and a different ending.

356
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:20,356
And two point two three, just a little further out. Minus two point two zero.

357
00:23:20,356 --> 00:23:24,530
Then plus two point zero two. Then minus one point one zero.

358
00:23:24,530 --> 00:23:29,260
It takes three flips to get inside, it arrives on the negative side,

359
00:23:29,260 --> 00:23:31,000
and it ends at minus one.

360
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:34,499
So the starts in the gap do not all end the same way.

361
00:23:34,499 --> 00:23:38,064
What matters is how many flips it takes to get inside.

362
00:23:38,064 --> 00:23:42,091
Every start here is positive, and each flip changes the sign.

363
00:23:42,091 --> 00:23:47,307
After an odd number of flips the value arrives negative, and ends at minus one.

364
00:23:47,307 --> 00:23:51,466
After an even number it arrives positive, and ends at plus one.

365
00:23:51,466 --> 00:23:56,335
Three starts are not the whole story, so here is every start at once.

366
00:23:56,335 --> 00:24:00,780
Take each start from zero to two point four, run the iteration,

367
00:24:00,780 --> 00:24:02,897
and color it by where it ends.

368
00:24:02,897 --> 00:24:07,766
Teal means plus one, gold means minus one, and red means it explodes.

369
00:24:07,766 --> 00:24:12,844
Below square root of three it is all teal, as we showed. The gap begins gold.

370
00:24:12,844 --> 00:24:16,339
That is where two point zero sits, with its one flip.

371
00:24:16,339 --> 00:24:20,098
Then comes a thin teal stripe, which holds two point two.

372
00:24:20,098 --> 00:24:23,395
And then more stripes, each thinner than the last,

373
00:24:23,395 --> 00:24:26,033
squeezed up against square root of five.

374
00:24:26,033 --> 00:24:31,334
So each stripe is a flip count. One flip, two flips, three flips, and so on.

375
00:24:31,334 --> 00:24:33,147
Two things are still open.

376
00:24:33,147 --> 00:24:37,471
Where exactly does one flip turn into two, and two into three?

377
00:24:37,471 --> 00:24:40,749
And do these stripes really fill the whole gap,

378
00:24:40,749 --> 00:24:43,400
all the way up to square root of five?

379
00:24:44,400 --> 00:24:48,795
Following the sign and the size together on this picture gets messy.

380
00:24:48,795 --> 00:24:51,639
But the mirror rule lets us take them apart.

381
00:24:51,639 --> 00:24:56,616
A negative value moves exactly like its positive twin, with the sign flipped.

382
00:24:56,616 --> 00:25:01,463
So the size follows a rule of its own. The next size is the size of p of x.

383
00:25:01,463 --> 00:25:03,596
And the sign we can simply count.

384
00:25:03,596 --> 00:25:07,733
Every step that starts outside square root of three is one flip.

385
00:25:07,733 --> 00:25:12,911
So fold the picture. We only need positive sizes, so look at the right half.

386
00:25:12,911 --> 00:25:16,863
Wherever the curve dips below the axis, flip that part up.

387
00:25:16,863 --> 00:25:19,520
This folded curve answers one question.

388
00:25:19,520 --> 00:25:23,200
If the size is x now, what is the size after one step?

389
00:25:23,200 --> 00:25:27,528
Now compare it with the diagonal, where the size would stay the same.

390
00:25:27,528 --> 00:25:30,852
Between square root of three and square root of five,

391
00:25:30,852 --> 00:25:33,299
the folded curve is below the diagonal.

392
00:25:33,299 --> 00:25:36,811
The size after the step is smaller than the size before.

393
00:25:36,811 --> 00:25:41,328
Beyond square root of five it is above the diagonal, and the size grows.

394
00:25:41,328 --> 00:25:43,900
They meet exactly at square root of five.

395
00:25:43,900 --> 00:25:48,577
And the cobweb works on the folded picture too. Start at two point two.

396
00:25:48,577 --> 00:25:51,607
Up to the folded curve, at two point zero two.

397
00:25:51,607 --> 00:25:56,482
Across to the diagonal, and then to the curve again, at one point one one.

398
00:25:56,482 --> 00:25:59,512
Now we are inside, and the size climbs to one.

399
00:25:59,512 --> 00:26:03,201
Two of these steps started outside square root of three.

400
00:26:03,201 --> 00:26:06,100
So two flips, and the sign ends up positive.

401
00:26:06,100 --> 00:26:10,228
Now zoom in on the gap, and stretch it sideways so we can see.

402
00:26:10,228 --> 00:26:12,892
When does a start need exactly one flip?

403
00:26:12,892 --> 00:26:18,153
When its first step already lands inside, at a size below square root of three.

404
00:26:18,153 --> 00:26:20,817
So draw that level as a horizontal line.

405
00:26:20,817 --> 00:26:24,013
The folded curve climbs through it at one point.

406
00:26:24,013 --> 00:26:26,544
Call that start b one, b for boundary.

407
00:26:26,544 --> 00:26:29,873
A start to the left of b one lands below the line.

408
00:26:29,873 --> 00:26:34,801
One flip, and it is inside, on the negative side, so it ends at minus one.

409
00:26:34,801 --> 00:26:36,466
This is the first stripe.

410
00:26:36,466 --> 00:26:41,491
Where is b one exactly? Its size after one step must be square root of three.

411
00:26:41,491 --> 00:26:46,125
With the size factor, that is b, times b squared minus three, over two,

412
00:26:46,125 --> 00:26:51,345
equals square root of three. Multiply by two, and we get b cubed, minus three b,

413
00:26:51,345 --> 00:26:56,240
equals two times square root of three, which is about three point four six.

414
00:26:56,240 --> 00:26:58,785
This has no tidy answer, so try values.

415
00:26:58,785 --> 00:27:02,635
Two point one gives two point nine six, which is too small.

416
00:27:02,635 --> 00:27:06,485
Two point two gives four point zero five, which is too big.

417
00:27:06,485 --> 00:27:10,466
Closing in between them gives about two point one four eight.

418
00:27:10,466 --> 00:27:12,086
That fits what we saw.

419
00:27:12,086 --> 00:27:17,756
One point eight and two point zero are both below b one, in the first stripe,

420
00:27:17,756 --> 00:27:22,027
and both ended at minus one. Two point two is above b one.

421
00:27:22,027 --> 00:27:26,666
Its first step did not get inside, and it needed a second flip.

422
00:27:27,667 --> 00:27:31,724
Now the starts above b one. Their first step does not get inside.

423
00:27:31,724 --> 00:27:34,595
But we do not have to follow them all the way.

424
00:27:34,595 --> 00:27:38,901
Suppose the first step lands, in size, somewhere in the first stripe.

425
00:27:38,901 --> 00:27:44,020
We already know the first stripe. From there it takes one more flip to get inside.

426
00:27:44,020 --> 00:27:46,267
So such a start flips exactly twice.

427
00:27:46,267 --> 00:27:50,730
Which starts are those? Read it off the picture. On the vertical axis,

428
00:27:50,730 --> 00:27:55,576
the first stripe is the band of sizes from square root of three up to b one.

429
00:27:55,576 --> 00:27:58,254
The folded curve passes through that band.

430
00:27:58,254 --> 00:28:03,610
It enters at b one, and it leaves at a new point, where its height is exactly b one.

431
00:28:03,610 --> 00:28:05,012
Call that point b two.

432
00:28:05,012 --> 00:28:09,157
So every start between b one and b two lands in the first stripe.

433
00:28:09,157 --> 00:28:14,067
Two flips, an even number, so it ends at plus one. This is the second stripe.

434
00:28:14,067 --> 00:28:18,169
Check it with two point two. It sits between b one and b two.

435
00:28:18,169 --> 00:28:21,060
Its first step has size two point zero two.

436
00:28:21,060 --> 00:28:25,566
And two point zero two lies between square root of three and b one,

437
00:28:25,566 --> 00:28:29,332
in the first stripe. So one more flip, and it is inside.

438
00:28:29,332 --> 00:28:33,300
That is the two flips we counted, and it ended at plus one.

439
00:28:33,300 --> 00:28:35,134
And the same step works again.

440
00:28:35,134 --> 00:28:39,966
Starts that land in the second stripe need one flip more than the second stripe

441
00:28:39,966 --> 00:28:44,552
does, so three flips. The curve leaves that band where its height is b two.

442
00:28:44,552 --> 00:28:49,628
Call that point b three. The starts between b two and b three are the third stripe,

443
00:28:49,628 --> 00:28:54,521
and they end at minus one. Each new stripe is carried onto the stripe before it,

444
00:28:54,521 --> 00:28:56,600
so it needs exactly one more flip.

445
00:28:56,600 --> 00:29:00,024
Each edge is found like b one, by trying values.

446
00:29:00,024 --> 00:29:05,587
b one is about two point one four eight. b two is about two point two two one.

447
00:29:05,587 --> 00:29:08,583
b three is about two point two three four.

448
00:29:08,583 --> 00:29:13,933
They creep up toward square root of five, which is two point two three six.

449
00:29:13,933 --> 00:29:19,140
So here are all the stripes on one line. Stripe number n flips n times.

450
00:29:19,140 --> 00:29:23,467
An odd n ends at minus one, and an even n ends at plus one.

451
00:29:23,467 --> 00:29:26,033
That is the striped picture we saw.

452
00:29:26,033 --> 00:29:29,295
The stripes pile up against square root of five.

453
00:29:29,295 --> 00:29:33,033
Zoom in, and the same pattern shows up again and again.

454
00:29:33,033 --> 00:29:35,641
Why do the stripes get thin so quickly?

455
00:29:35,641 --> 00:29:39,051
Near square root of five the folded curve is steep.

456
00:29:39,051 --> 00:29:44,668
From two point two to two point two three, the start moves by zero point zero three.

457
00:29:44,668 --> 00:29:50,018
But the size after the step moves from two point zero two to two point two zero,

458
00:29:50,018 --> 00:29:53,762
which is zero point one eight. That is six times as far.

459
00:29:53,762 --> 00:29:57,373
So one step stretches a stripe to six times its width.

460
00:29:57,373 --> 00:30:02,054
And the stretched stripe has to fit exactly onto the stripe before it.

461
00:30:02,054 --> 00:30:06,267
So each stripe is only about a sixth as wide as the one before.

462
00:30:07,267 --> 00:30:11,551
One question is still open. Do the stripes really fill the whole gap?

463
00:30:11,551 --> 00:30:15,276
Or is there a last piece, right next to square root of five,

464
00:30:15,276 --> 00:30:19,002
that no stripe ever reaches? Look at how we found the edges.

465
00:30:19,002 --> 00:30:22,852
Here is the corner of the picture next to square root of five.

466
00:30:22,852 --> 00:30:27,571
Start at the height square root of three, and go across to the folded curve.

467
00:30:27,571 --> 00:30:32,725
That is b one. Go up to the diagonal, and across to the curve again. That is b two.

468
00:30:32,725 --> 00:30:37,133
The edges are a staircase, squeezed between the curve and the diagonal.

469
00:30:37,133 --> 00:30:40,092
Every step of the staircase moves to the right,

470
00:30:40,092 --> 00:30:45,316
and it can never get past square root of five. So the edges close in on some point.

471
00:30:45,316 --> 00:30:48,149
At that point the staircase has no room left,

472
00:30:48,149 --> 00:30:52,241
which means the curve touches the diagonal there. And in the gap,

473
00:30:52,241 --> 00:30:57,402
the only place where the curve touches the diagonal is square root of five itself.

474
00:30:57,402 --> 00:31:01,242
So the edges come as close to square root of five as we like.

475
00:31:01,242 --> 00:31:05,082
Every start below square root of five is passed by some edge,

476
00:31:05,082 --> 00:31:08,733
so it lies in some stripe. The stripes fill the whole gap.

477
00:31:08,733 --> 00:31:11,756
And what about the edges themselves? Take b one.

478
00:31:11,756 --> 00:31:15,472
Its first step lands exactly on minus square root of three.

479
00:31:15,472 --> 00:31:20,698
And we know what happens at square root of three. The next step gives exactly zero,

480
00:31:20,698 --> 00:31:25,044
and by the mirror rule the same holds for minus square root of three.

481
00:31:25,044 --> 00:31:27,500
So b one ends on zero, and stays there.

482
00:31:27,500 --> 00:31:29,303
b two lands on minus b one,

483
00:31:29,303 --> 00:31:33,710
and from there it follows the path of b one with the sign flipped.

484
00:31:33,710 --> 00:31:38,051
So it takes one step more. And b three takes one step more again.

485
00:31:38,051 --> 00:31:42,858
Every edge reaches plus or minus square root of three after a few steps,

486
00:31:42,858 --> 00:31:47,733
and then it sits on zero forever. An edge never reaches one or minus one.

487
00:31:47,733 --> 00:31:50,027
Should that worry us? Not much.

488
00:31:50,027 --> 00:31:54,540
Each edge is a single point, and zero is the top of the hill.

489
00:31:54,540 --> 00:31:57,278
Move the start by the tiniest amount,

490
00:31:57,278 --> 00:32:01,052
and it is inside a stripe on one side or the other,

491
00:32:01,052 --> 00:32:04,233
and it rolls down to plus one or minus one.

492
00:32:04,233 --> 00:32:07,219
Now we can answer the question we started with.

493
00:32:07,219 --> 00:32:11,476
Where does a positive sigma end up when we apply p again and again?

494
00:32:11,476 --> 00:32:16,433
Here is the full answer to part (e), from left to right along the number line.

495
00:32:16,433 --> 00:32:20,967
Below square root of three, the first step lands between zero and one,

496
00:32:20,967 --> 00:32:23,558
and from there sigma climbs to plus one.

497
00:32:23,558 --> 00:32:27,833
Exactly at square root of three, it lands on zero and stays there.

498
00:32:27,833 --> 00:32:31,216
Between square root of three and square root of five,

499
00:32:31,216 --> 00:32:34,153
each step flips the sign and shrinks the size,

500
00:32:34,153 --> 00:32:37,153
until the value is inside square root of three.

501
00:32:37,153 --> 00:32:42,133
An odd number of flips ends at minus one, and an even number ends at plus one.

502
00:32:42,133 --> 00:32:46,666
That gives the stripes. Their edges are single points that end on zero.

503
00:32:46,666 --> 00:32:48,868
Exactly at square root of five,

504
00:32:48,868 --> 00:32:53,343
sigma jumps between plus and minus square root of five forever.

505
00:32:53,343 --> 00:32:58,599
And beyond square root of five, each step flips and grows, so it explodes.

506
00:32:58,599 --> 00:33:02,933
Out of all these cases, only the first one is what we wanted.

507
00:33:02,933 --> 00:33:05,756
We wanted every singular value to end at one,

508
00:33:05,756 --> 00:33:09,520
and that only happens for starts below square root of three.

509
00:33:09,520 --> 00:33:12,468
But a real W can have singular values anywhere,

510
00:33:12,468 --> 00:33:15,166
some of them far above square root of five.

511
00:33:15,166 --> 00:33:17,900
This one, at two point nine, would explode.

512
00:33:17,900 --> 00:33:21,587
And one that sits in a gold stripe would end at minus one,

513
00:33:21,587 --> 00:33:23,367
with its direction reversed.

514
00:33:23,367 --> 00:33:27,499
So before we iterate, we have to bring every singular value down.

515
00:33:27,499 --> 00:33:28,707
That is easy to do.

516
00:33:28,707 --> 00:33:33,348
Divide W by a number, and every singular value is divided by that number,

517
00:33:33,348 --> 00:33:36,272
while U and V transpose stay exactly the same.

518
00:33:36,272 --> 00:33:39,833
Our target, U times V transpose, does not change at all.

519
00:33:39,833 --> 00:33:44,158
Which number? Dividing by the largest singular value would be ideal,

520
00:33:44,158 --> 00:33:46,894
because then everything is at one or below.

521
00:33:46,894 --> 00:33:52,047
But finding the largest singular value takes the very SVD we are trying to avoid.

522
00:33:52,047 --> 00:33:55,419
There is a cheap stand-in, called the Frobenius norm.

523
00:33:55,419 --> 00:33:59,681
Square every entry of W, add them all up, and take the square root.

524
00:33:59,681 --> 00:34:04,643
That only needs the entries, so it costs almost nothing. And it is big enough.

525
00:34:04,643 --> 00:34:09,987
The sum of the squared entries always equals the sum of the squared singular values.

526
00:34:09,987 --> 00:34:13,868
That sum already contains the largest one squared, plus more.

527
00:34:13,868 --> 00:34:16,349
So its square root, the Frobenius norm,

528
00:34:16,349 --> 00:34:19,466
is never smaller than the largest singular value.

529
00:34:19,466 --> 00:34:23,843
Here the Frobenius norm comes out to about three point four five,

530
00:34:23,843 --> 00:34:27,816
a bit more than our largest singular value, two point nine.

531
00:34:27,816 --> 00:34:29,499
Divide every sigma by it.

532
00:34:29,499 --> 00:34:34,814
Two point nine becomes zero point eight four, and the others are smaller still.

533
00:34:34,814 --> 00:34:38,245
Now every singular value sits between zero and one,

534
00:34:38,245 --> 00:34:40,533
safely below square root of three.

535
00:34:40,533 --> 00:34:45,961
Now apply our matrix step, three halves W, minus one half W, W transpose, W,

536
00:34:45,961 --> 00:34:49,818
again and again. It needs nothing but matrix products.

537
00:34:49,818 --> 00:34:53,818
And each time, every singular value goes through p once.

538
00:34:53,818 --> 00:34:58,890
So each singular value climbs to plus one. The small ones start slowly,

539
00:34:58,890 --> 00:35:03,818
growing by about one and a half times per step as zero point one did,

540
00:35:03,818 --> 00:35:08,675
but they all arrive. And all along, U and V transpose never changed.

541
00:35:08,675 --> 00:35:13,533
So the middle is now all ones, and W has become U times V transpose.

542
00:35:13,533 --> 00:35:16,149
The ellipse has become a circle.

543
00:35:16,149 --> 00:35:22,199
All it took was matrix products, and a cubic built from two simple wishes.

544
00:35:22,199 --> 00:35:27,261
So the whole method is two lines. Divide W by its Frobenius norm, once.

545
00:35:27,261 --> 00:35:29,899
Then apply the step, again and again.

546
00:35:29,899 --> 00:35:32,600
And where does a singular value go?

547
00:35:32,600 --> 00:35:36,999
After that first division, every one of them goes to one.

